{
  "notes": {
    "calculus-section-17-4-1": "Coefficient matching gives (n+1)cₙ₊₁=cₙ, hence cₙ=C/n!.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-2": "c₁=0 and (n+1)cₙ₊₁=cₙ₋₁. Odd coefficients vanish and even coefficients are C/(2^n n!).",
    "calculus-section-17-4-3": "c₁=c₂=0 and (n+1)cₙ₊₁=cₙ₋₂. Only powers divisible by three remain.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-4": "(n+2)cₙ−3(n+1)cₙ₊₁=0 gives cₙ=c₀(n+1)/3^n. The zero solution extends everywhere; nonzero solutions have a pole at x=3.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-5": "The x^n coefficient is (n+2)(n+1)cₙ₊₂+(n+1)cₙ. The recurrence and two free starting coefficients determine every coefficient.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-6": "(n+2)(n+1)cₙ₊₂=cₙ; even and odd coefficients propagate independently. Both sums start at n=0.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-7": "The differential equation gives cₙ₊₂=(n+1)cₙ₊₁/(n+2), so cₙ=c₁/n for n≥1. The series converges conditionally at −1, diverges at +1; termwise differential operations are justified inside |x|<1.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-8": "Match y″=xy: 2c₂=0 and (n+3)(n+2)cₙ₊₃=cₙ. The resulting two power series are entire.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-9": "c₀=1,c₁=0; cₙ₊₂=cₙ/(n+2), so all odd coefficients vanish.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-10": "From y″=−x²y, the constant and linear coefficients vanish and the recurrence steps by four.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-11": "The x^(n+1) coefficient of x²y′+xy is (n+1)cₙ; use both initial values and propagate the recurrence.",
    "calculus-section-17-4-12": "Coefficient matching yields n²cₙ=−cₙ₋₂, c₀=1,c₁=0. For |x|≤5, terms decrease from n≥2, so the T₂₀ tail is at most 5²²/[4¹¹(11!)²]<4×10^−6. Plot successive even polynomials; the curve itself is not automatically graded."
  },
